from mk import mk
mk("3q-o772IYQY","Celestine - A Realtime Granular Dream Machine (Full Walkthrough)",
 "live-rolling-buffer-granular-grain-behaviour-modes-mosaic-glide-bloom-tide",
 "Live rolling-buffer granular processor with per-grain behaviour modes: interval 'mosaic' clouds, per-grain pitch glide, reverse+wow smear, ghost-grain bloom, LFO tide, fixed-position constellation patterns and orbiting read head",
 "Turn a constantly recording one-bar buffer into a real-time granular effect for pads, keys, vocals and guitar (insert with dry/wet or 100% wet send). It uses the standard grain controls (size, density, spray around the playhead, pitch in perfect-fifth steps, jitter, spread, stereo scatter, reverse chance, top-end smoothing), a tempo-synced grain trigger/size option, a quantised or auto-moving playhead, and a library of grain-scheduling behaviours a Web Audio granulator can implement: mosaic (grains pitched into chordal interval sets: fifths/major/minor/discordant), glide (each grain sweeps up in pitch over its own length with a curve), reverie (all grains reversed with tape-style wow), bloom (each grain spawns a later, quieter, slightly detuned ghost grain), tide (a global LFO swells and recedes playback rate), stars (grains drawn from fixed buffer positions in forward/reverse/pendulum/random patterns, looping each bar) and orbit (grains circling the write head with width and 'slop'). Delta vs our granular-buffer-core and frozen-buffer skills: live write/read buffer and the per-grain behaviour modes.",
 ["Granular engine reading a circular buffer that is continuously written (Web Audio: AudioWorklet ring buffer + grain scheduler, implementation note)","Tempo clock for synced grains","Optional wavefolder, bitcrusher and long hall reverb after the grains"],
 [("source","audio","Melodic material (keys, vocals, guitar, pads) or percussion",True),("buffer_length","string","One bar of the current tempo",False),("mode","string","grain | mosaic | glide | reverie | bloom | tide | stars | orbit",True)],
 [("granular_texture","audio","Real-time granular cloud derived from what is playing right now")],
 ["The buffer is a fleeting rolling recording, not storage: pausing record ('live' off) holds the content only until the next playback start.","Read and write heads must not collide inside the ring buffer or you get glitches. Keep a guard distance between them (developer's stated engine problem; the fix is not described).","Mode behaviours are described qualitatively; parameter amounts are by ear.","Pricing, story and shop links in the source are omitted."],
 [("Insert the processor on a track (balance dry/wet) or on a send at 100% wet. A one-bar ring buffer records whatever plays, and grains are read from it continuously.","routing","Grains follow the live input with no pre-recorded sample"),
  ("Set the base cloud: size (grain length), density (grains at once, sparse to dense), spray (how far from the playhead grains may be fetched; 0 = exactly at the playhead), pitch in perfect-fifth steps, jitter (slight per-grain pitch warble, good for cloudy drones), spread (any pitch per grain, extreme), stereo (grains scattered around the field; presenter keeps it high), reverse chance, and smooth (roll off top end if grains get clicky).","grain","A dense or sparse cloud with controlled pitch and stereo scatter"),
  ("Playhead: scrub manually, or quantise it to eight positions (eighth notes in the one-bar buffer) to land on downbeat transients, or set a move rate (slow/fast, forward or reverse) to scan automatically.","playhead","Grains drawn from chosen rhythmic positions or a moving scan"),
  ("Sync sub-mode: when synced, grains fire on time divisions (e.g. 16ths) and size switches to note lengths. Each behaviour mode below has a free and a synced variant.","sync","Grains snap to the grid; percussive, rhythmic output"),
  ("Mosaic: pitch successive grains into chordal intervals (a couple at root, then +7, +12 …) with a soft bloom and slight lo-fi. Pick an interval set: fifths, major, minor or discordant. Reduce density; add jitter or spread. Good for pads with lots of reverb.","mosaic","Harmonic cloud chords built from the input"),
  ("Glide: each grain glides up in pitch (micro-portamento). Glide depth sets the interval; grain size sets how long it takes, and a shape control sets the curve. Long grains give a smooth effect; percussive input makes a texture generator.","glide","Upward-gliding grain cloud"),
  ("Reverie: force all grains into reverse and add tape-like wow/flutter (wow depth control) with a slight lo-fi tone; longer sizes give stretched tape-echo smears. Reverse chance has no further effect here.","reverie","Dreamy reversed smear"),
  ("Bloom: every triggered grain spawns a secondary ghost grain that enters slightly later, quieter and subtly detuned. Raise density and size for overlapping halos.","bloom","Lush, reverb-like granular halo"),
  ("Tide: a global LFO makes all grains swell and recede in playback rate together. Long size = ambient breathing; short size = tremolo-like.","tide","Organic push-pull breathing of the texture"),
  ("Stars: ignore the heads and read grains from fixed positions in the buffer. Spray becomes a pattern selector (forward, reverse, pendulum, random), which cycles each bar into recognisable melodic or rhythmic fragment loops.","stars","Repeatable fragment patterns per bar"),
  ("Orbit: grains circle the write head in a spiralling orbit. Spray becomes orbit width, plus a slop control for imperfection/lo-fi. Works best at slow tempos and lower densities and plays alongside the dry signal like its own loop.","orbit","Rotating texture revolving around the 'present' audio"),
  ("Finish inside the grain path: a wavefolder or bitcrusher with its own mix for grit, then a simple hall reverb (decay + mix); long decay feels almost frozen. Revisit playhead/spray/sync after picking a mode, since each changes the mode's result a lot.","fx","Saturated or spacious finished texture")],
 ["With spray at 0 and move off, grains come only from the playhead position.","Synced mode places grains on the chosen division, audibly on the grid.","Mosaic output contains the chosen interval set (e.g. root/+7/+12) when fed a single note.","Bloom shows delayed, quieter duplicate grains; tide shows periodic rate swells."],
 ["granular","real-time","ring-buffer","grain-modes","pitch-shift","reverse","pad","texture","web-audio"],
 note="Developer walkthrough of a commercial Max for Live device. Long personal/pricing preamble and shop CTA dropped; modes kept as portable grain-scheduling recipes.")
